# Work behind a fenced index

For a project whose packages come from, and go to, your organisation's Artifactory instead of
PyPI. Nothing in the project changes for it: the fence is a set of variables your organisation
sets once{% if git_hosting_service == 'gitlab' %}, at group level,{% else %} on the repository,{% endif %} and every project under it inherits. The same
pipeline file, Dockerfile and tasks serve a project in the open with every variable unset.

## The variables

| Variable | Set where | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `UV_DEFAULT_INDEX` | {% if git_hosting_service == 'gitlab' %}the group{% else %}a repository variable{% endif %}, and your shell | uv resolves and installs from this index instead of PyPI, including the lock generation wrote. The URL is the index's simple API. |
| `UV_PUBLISH_URL` | {% if git_hosting_service == 'gitlab' %}the group, scoped to the `pypi` environment{% else %}a repository secret{% endif %} | `release.publish` uploads here, with credentials and without attestations, as [Publish to a private index](publish-to-a-private-index.md) describes. |
| `JFROG_URL`, `JFROG_PROJECT`, `JFROG_AUDIENCE` | {% if git_hosting_service == 'gitlab' %}the group{% else %}repository variables{% endif %} | Each job mints an ID token for the audience and exchanges it at `JFROG_URL/access/api/v1/oidc/token` for a token scoped to the project, as user `<project>-ci`. `JFROG_PROVIDER` names the identity provider Artifactory verifies against; `{{ git_hosting_service }}` unless set. No stored secret anywhere. |
| `PIP_INDEX_URL` | {% if git_hosting_service == 'gitlab' %}the group{% else %}not needed{% endif %} | {% if git_hosting_service == 'gitlab' %}The plan job installs uv with pip before the launcher exists; pip reads this, not `UV_DEFAULT_INDEX`. Same value.{% else %}Nothing on GitHub installs with pip.{% endif %} |
| `UV_PYTHON_INSTALL_MIRROR` | {% if git_hosting_service == 'gitlab' %}the group{% else %}a repository variable{% endif %}, and your shell | Where uv fetches the interpreters the tox matrix needs, which it otherwise downloads from GitHub. A generic remote in Artifactory in front of `github.com/astral-sh/python-build-standalone/releases/download` serves them. |
{%- if git_hosting_service == 'github' %}

GitHub injects none of these on its own: every workflow names them in its `env:` from `vars`,
so a variable the workflows do not name never reaches a job. An unset one arrives as an empty
string, which uv and the tasks read as unset. Repository and organisation variables are not
secrets, so a pull request from a fork sees them too, and fails on its first fetch for want of
a token; a fenced project does not take pull requests from forks.

Two things the GitHub pipeline reaches for that the fence may not cover: the deps image is
pushed to and pulled from `ghcr.io`, and `setup-uv` fetches uv from GitHub's releases. On
github.com with hosted runners both are reachable. On self-hosted runners inside the fence,
the registry the image goes to is a decision the template has not taken; see the fence
report in the template's `reports/` for the question it raises.
{%- endif %}
{%- if git_hosting_service == 'github' %}
| `INDEX_USERNAME`, `INDEX_PASSWORD` | repository secrets | A token the index accepts, for a project that would rather not map an OIDC identity. Set, they win over the exchange everywhere. |
{%- endif %}
{%- if git_hosting_service == 'gitlab' %}

`JFROG_AUDIENCE` has a placeholder in `.gitlab-ci.yml`, because GitLab refuses an `id_tokens:`
entry with an empty audience and a project in the open has none to give. Variables set in the
pipeline file rank below group variables, so the group's value wins wherever it is set.
{%- endif %}

## What the pipeline does with them

{% if git_hosting_service == 'gitlab' -%}
- **Every job** mints the ID token through the pipeline's `default:` block.
- **`plan-deps-image`** exchanges it and writes `INDEX_URL`, `INDEX_USERNAME` and
  `INDEX_PASSWORD` into the dotenv it already hands the kaniko job, which passes them as build
  arguments. The artifact expires after an hour.
- **`build-deps-image`** builds with them. The builder stage of `Dockerfile.deps` is the only
  stage that sees them; the pushed image carries the synced environment and no trace, which
  `preflight` checks with `container.audit`.
- **`preflight`, `secure` and `publish`** run `index.login` first, which exchanges the token
  and writes a netrc for the index host, so the tox matrix, `uv build` and the audit's
  resolution authenticate for the rest of the job. `publish` then fills `UV_PUBLISH_USERNAME`
  and `UV_PUBLISH_PASSWORD` beside the `UV_PUBLISH_URL` the group set.
{%- else -%}
- **Every workflow** hands the five variables down as environment, and every job that fetches
  may write an OIDC token.
- **`build-deps-image`** mints its ID token from the Actions endpoint, exchanges it, and names
  the three `INDEX_*` values on the build command line while supplying them through the
  environment, so the echoed command shows none. The builder stage of `Dockerfile.deps` is
  the only stage that sees them; `container.audit` runs right after the push and checks that
  they stayed there.
- **`preflight`, `secure` and `publish`** run `index.login` first, which mints and exchanges
  the token and writes a netrc for the index host, so the tox matrix, `uv build` and the
  audit's resolution authenticate for the rest of the job. `publish` fills
  `UV_PUBLISH_USERNAME` and `UV_PUBLISH_PASSWORD` beside the `UV_PUBLISH_URL` secret.
- **A pull request from a fork** gets no OIDC token and no variables, so its jobs run in the
  open and fail on the first fetch. A fenced project does not take pull requests from forks.
{%- endif %}

## On your machine

Put `UV_DEFAULT_INDEX` and `UV_PYTHON_INSTALL_MIRROR` in your shell profile, then
`uv auth login <host>` with your own Artifactory identity token, which uv stores for every
request to that host. For `./workflow.cmd container.build` set `INDEX_USERNAME` and
`INDEX_PASSWORD` the same way for the one command; the build names them and never echoes them.
Generation runs `uv lock` with the same variables and credentials, so a project generated
behind the fence locks against the index from its first commit.

## What the fence does not cover

- **The advisory database.** `secure.audit` asks pypi.org or osv.dev about each package, and
  pip-audit has no way to ask anything else. Artifactory does not proxy it. The audit needs
  egress to one of the two, or runs where it has it.
- **The docs inventories.** `document.build` fetches the Python and typing-extensions object
  inventories named in `properdocs.yml` for cross-references. Behind a fence, drop the two
  `inventories` entries or point them at copies you host.
- **The base image.** `Dockerfile.deps` builds on an image from ghcr.io, pinned by digest in
  `pyproject.toml`. A Docker remote in Artifactory serves it under another name; edit
  `[tool.docker-versions] base-image` to that name and keep the digest.
- **The render hook's uv lookup.** Generation asks pypi.org for the newest uv to pin and
  falls back to the committed pin, saying so, when it cannot.

## The Artifactory side

Artifactory needs an OIDC integration for your {{ git_hosting_service | capitalize }}, with
the audience in `JFROG_AUDIENCE`, and an identity mapping from this project's token claims to
the `<project>-ci` user with read on the index and deploy on the upload repository. Both are
your platform team's to set; this how-to only names what they map to.

